 "literal": "Hollow-tooth like thick-claw flat-foot / dark male elephant herd leader if comes having disappeared / rock crushed sugarcane joints like / withered single bamboo tall desert path having crossed / did they attain indeed wealth? loins / those beautiful lines to fade he who abandoned / hard-hearted person to become himself went land indeed",
 "translation": "Did he indeed attain the wealth he sought, in that land where he went, becoming so hard-hearted? He who abandoned you, causing the beautiful lines on your loins to fade, crossed the desert path where a single withered bamboo stands tall, looking like the joints of sugarcane crushed on a rock, and where the bear—with flat feet and thick claws like hollow teeth—hides away when the leader of the herd of dark male elephants approaches.",
 "modern": "உன்னுடைய அல்குலில் உள்ள அழகிய வரிகள் வாடிப் போகும்படி உன்னைக் கைவிட்டுச் சென்றவர், தான் ஒரு கல்நெஞ்சக்காரராக மாறிச் சென்ற அந்த நாட்டில், தான் தேடிச் சென்ற செல்வத்தை அடைந்துவிட்டாரோ? உளுத்த பற்களைப் போன்ற பருத்த நகங்களையும் பரந்த அடிகளையும் உடைய கரடி, கரிய ஆண் யானைக் கூட்டத்தின் தலைவன் வரும்போது அஞ்சி மறையும்படியான, பாறையில் நசுங்கிய கரும்பின் கணுக்களைப் போல வாடிய ஒற்றை மூங்கில் உயர்ந்து நிற்கும் பாலைவன வழியைக் கடந்து சென்றாரே!",
 "explanation": "The friend (tōḻi) is speaking to the heroine (talaivi) to console her during the hero's absence, while also expressing her own anxiety about the harshness of the path he has taken and his apparent coldness. She describes the desolate desert (pālai) landscape where a single withered bamboo stands, resembling sugarcane crushed on a rock, symbolizing the heroine's own broken and withered state. In this wilderness, even the powerful bear, with its flat feet and sharp claws, hides in fear when the leader of the wild elephant herd approaches, highlighting the extreme danger of the route. The friend wonders aloud if the hero, who became hard-hearted enough to leave his beloved to pine until her beauty faded, has actually achieved the wealth for which he undertook such a perilous journey. The key words are 'சுரன்' (desert path), 'துறந்தோர்' (he who abandoned), and 'வன்பர்' (hard-hearted person).",
